mod('organelles','Cell organelles & compartments',
 'A eukaryotic cell is a set of rooms, each with its own job. Keeping reactions in separate compartments is what lets a cell run many incompatible processes at once.',
 ['The <b>nucleus</b> holds the DNA. <b>Ribosomes</b> (not membrane-bound) build proteins; those on the <b>rough ER</b> make proteins for export or membranes, and the <b>smooth ER</b> makes lipids and detoxifies. The <b>Golgi</b> modifies, sorts and packages proteins into vesicles. <b>Mitochondria</b> carry out most cellular respiration and have a double membrane with folded inner cristae. <b>Lysosomes</b> digest material with hydrolytic enzymes. Plant cells also have <b>chloroplasts</b> (photosynthesis), a large central <b>vacuole</b> and a cell wall.',
  'Compartments matter because each has its own conditions. Lysosome enzymes work at acidic pH that would damage the rest of the cell; the inner mitochondrial membrane holds a proton gradient. Prokaryotes have no membrane-bound organelles, but they do have ribosomes, a plasma membrane, DNA in a nucleoid and (usually) a cell wall.'],
 ('a cell was just a bag of chemistry','that compartments keep different reactions and conditions separate','why mitochondria and chloroplasts have membranes inside membranes','organelle structure and function'),
 [('nucleus','DNA; makes RNA'),('rough ER → Golgi → vesicle','protein export pathway'),('mitochondrion','ATP from respiration'),('chloroplast','photosynthesis')],
 [lab('og-lab','Find the organelle',canvas='og-canvas',aria='A eukaryotic cell with the chosen organelle highlighted.',
   seg=('og-sel','Organelle',[('nuc','nucleus'),('rer','rough ER'),('gol','Golgi'),('mit','mitochondrion'),('lys','lysosome'),('chl','chloroplast')]),
   out=[('og-fn','function','x'),('og-mem','membranes','a'),('og-where','found in','v')],own=True),
  stagelab('og-lab2','Following a secreted protein',canvas='og-canvas2',aria='A protein moves from a ribosome on the rough ER through a vesicle to the Golgi and out of the cell.',p='ps',n=5)],
 [('Pick the mitochondrion. Why does it have two membranes? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#og-sel [data-k=mit]"]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','The double membrane (with the inner one folded into cristae) creates two compartments and a large surface for the electron transport chain; it also reflects its origin as an engulfed bacterium.'),
  ('Step through the secretion pathway to the Golgi. What happens there? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#ps-st",3]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','The protein is modified (for example by adding carbohydrate), sorted and packaged into a vesicle that carries it to the plasma membrane.')],
 ('Insulin is a protein secreted by pancreas cells. List the organelles it passes through in order.',[
  '<b>Made.</b> Translated by a ribosome on the rough ER; the growing chain enters the ER lumen.',
  '<b>Transported.</b> A transport vesicle buds off the ER and fuses with the Golgi.',
  '<b>Processed and packaged.</b> The Golgi modifies it and packages it in a secretory vesicle.',
  '<b>Secreted.</b> The vesicle fuses with the plasma membrane and releases insulin by exocytosis.']),
 ['Saying ribosomes are membrane-bound organelles.','Thinking the Golgi makes proteins. It processes and sorts them.','Mixing up smooth ER (lipids, detox) and rough ER (ribosomes, protein).','Believing prokaryotes have no ribosomes. They do, just not membrane-bound organelles.','Putting chloroplasts in animal cells.'],
 ('Why compartments help',['Compartments increase efficiency by concentrating enzymes and substrates, and protect the cell from hazardous reactions. Membranes inside organelles add surface area for reactions: cristae in mitochondria, thylakoids in chloroplasts, folded ER.']),
 [('Which organelle modifies and packages proteins for secretion?',['Golgi apparatus','ribosome','lysosome','nucleolus'],0,'The Golgi modifies, sorts and packages proteins into vesicles.')])

mod('sav','Surface area to volume',
 'A cell must take in nutrients through its surface but needs them for its whole volume. As a cell grows, the volume outpaces the surface, and that limits cell size.',
 ['For a cube of side s: surface area = 6s², volume = s³, so SA:V = 6/s. For a sphere of radius r: SA = 4πr², V = (4/3)πr³, so SA:V = 3/r. In both cases SA:V <b>decreases</b> as the cell gets bigger. Doubling the side multiplies SA by 4 but volume by 8.',
  'This explains why cells are small, why gas exchange organs (lungs, gills, root hairs, villi) are folded or branched to add surface area, and why large organisms need transport systems. A cell of any shape exchanges materials faster when its SA:V is higher, so a flattened or elongated cell does better than a sphere of the same volume.'],
 ('bigger cells just did the same things on a larger scale','that exchange scales with surface but need scales with volume','why cells stay small and why exchange surfaces are folded','surface area to volume calculations'),
 [('cube: SA = 6s², V = s³','SA:V = 6/s'),('sphere: SA = 4πr², V = (4/3)πr³','SA:V = 3/r'),('rectangular solid: SA = 2(lw + lh + wh), V = lwh','from the sheet'),('bigger cell → smaller SA:V','limits size')],
 [lab('sv-lab','Grow a cell',canvas='sv-canvas',aria='A cell drawn at the chosen size and a graph of SA:V against size with a marker at the current size.',
   seg=('sv-sh','Shape',[('cube','cube'),('sph','sphere')]),
   ctl=[('sv-s','Size (side or radius)',1,10,.5,3)],
   out=[('sv-sa','surface area','x'),('sv-v','volume','a'),('sv-r','SA : V','v'),('sv-n','can it exchange fast enough?','')],own=True)],
 [('Double the size from 2 to 4 (cube). What happens to SA, V and SA:V? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#sv-sh [data-k=cube]"],["#sv-s",2]) + '\'>Size 2</button>','','SA goes 24 → 96 (×4), V goes 8 → 64 (×8), so SA:V falls from 3 to 1.5 (÷2).'),
  ('Switch to a sphere at radius 3. Compare its SA:V with a cube of side 3. <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#sv-sh [data-k=sph]"],["#sv-s",3]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','Sphere: 3/3 = 1.0; cube side 3: 6/3 = 2.0. They are measured differently: the sphere of radius 3 is much bigger than a cube of side 3.')],
 ('Cell 1 is a cube of side 2 µm and Cell 2 is a cube of side 4 µm. Which is better at exchanging materials and by what factor?',[
  '<b>Cell 1.</b> SA = 6 × 2² = 24 µm², V = 8 µm³, SA:V = 3.',
  '<b>Cell 2.</b> SA = 6 × 4² = 96 µm², V = 64 µm³, SA:V = 1.5.',
  '<b>Compare.</b> Cell 1 has twice the SA:V, so it is better at exchange (the smaller cell).']),
 ['Concluding that a bigger cell has more surface area per unit volume.','Mixing up SA:V with total surface area.','Applying 6/s to a sphere.','Forgetting units: µm², µm³ and µm⁻¹.','Thinking cells fix the problem by getting bigger. Folding, flattening or branching raises SA:V instead.'],
 ('Applying SA:V on the exam',['A typical FRQ gives dimensions, asks for SA:V of two cells, then asks which is better at exchange or how an adaptation (microvilli, root hairs, branching) helps. Show the calculation, compare the ratios, and tie it to diffusion distance and exchange rate.']),
 [('If the side of a cube doubles, its volume changes by a factor of',['8','2','4','6'],0,'V = s³, so doubling s gives 2³ = 8 times the volume.')])

mod('membrane','Membrane structure & permeability',
 'A cell membrane is a thin oily film with proteins floating in it. It is selective because of what it is made of, not because it has holes.',
 ['The membrane is a <b>phospholipid bilayer</b>: each phospholipid has a hydrophilic head and two hydrophobic tails, so in water the tails hide in the middle. Proteins are embedded (integral) or attached (peripheral); carbohydrates on the outer surface help cells recognize each other. The pieces move around within the layer, which is why it is called a <b>fluid mosaic</b>. Cholesterol (in animal cells) buffers fluidity: it keeps the membrane from packing too tightly when cold and from becoming too loose when warm.',
  'Because the middle is nonpolar, small nonpolar molecules (O₂, CO₂) cross easily. Small polar molecules like water cross slowly (aquaporins speed it up). Ions and large polar molecules (glucose) need <b>channel or carrier proteins</b>. Very large particles move by <b>endocytosis</b> and <b>exocytosis</b>.'],
 ('a membrane was a barrier with holes','that selectivity comes from the nonpolar interior and from specific proteins','why oxygen passes freely but ions need help','phospholipids, proteins, cholesterol and permeability'),
 [('phospholipid: hydrophilic head, hydrophobic tails','forms a bilayer'),('small nonpolar → crosses directly','O₂, CO₂'),('ions, glucose → protein needed','channels, carriers'),('cholesterol buffers fluidity','temperature effect')],
 [lab('me-lab','Membrane fluidity',canvas='me-canvas',aria='A phospholipid bilayer whose molecules move more or less depending on temperature and cholesterol.',
   seg=('me-chol','Cholesterol',[('no','absent'),('yes','present')]),
   ctl=[('me-t','Temperature',0,50,1,25)],
   out=[('me-f','fluidity','x'),('me-s','what to expect','')],own=True),
  lab('me-lab2','What can cross?',canvas='me-canvas2',aria='A membrane with a molecule approaching and the route it takes, if any.',
   seg=('me-m','Molecule',[('o2','O₂'),('h2o','water'),('glu','glucose'),('na','Na⁺'),('pro','protein')]),
   out=[('me-how','route across','x'),('me-why','why','')],own=True)],
 [('Remove cholesterol and cool to 5 °C. What happens to fluidity? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#me-chol [data-k=no]"],["#me-t",5]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','Fluidity is very low: phospholipids pack tightly. With cholesterol present the membrane stays more fluid at this temperature.'),
  ('Choose Na⁺. Why can\'t it cross directly? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#me-m [data-k=na]"]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','Ions are charged and cannot pass through the hydrophobic interior. They need channel or carrier proteins.')],
 ('A protein is found to span the membrane, with a hydrophobic section in the middle and hydrophilic ends. Explain why.',[
  '<b>The interior is nonpolar.</b> The fatty acid tails form a hydrophobic core.',
  '<b>The middle section.</b> Its nonpolar amino acids interact with the tails, anchoring the protein in the bilayer.',
  '<b>The ends.</b> Polar and charged amino acids face the watery cytoplasm and extracellular fluid.']),
 ['Saying the membrane is static. It is a moving fluid mosaic.','Saying water crosses only through channels. Some crosses directly, slowly.','Thinking cholesterol makes the membrane rigid at all temperatures.','Putting the hydrophilic heads in the middle.','Forgetting the carbohydrate groups on the outside.'],
 ('Predicting permeability',['Ask two questions: is it small and nonpolar (crosses directly), and is it charged or large (needs a protein or vesicle)? Then decide whether energy is needed: moving down a gradient is passive, up a gradient needs ATP.']),
 [('Which molecule crosses the phospholipid bilayer most easily?',['oxygen','sodium ion','glucose','a protein'],0,'Oxygen is small and nonpolar and dissolves in the hydrophobic core.')])

mod('watpot','Water potential',
 'Water moves toward lower water potential. The number tells you the direction and, when you compare two places, which way the cell will swell or shrink.',
 ['<b>Water potential</b> (Ψ) measures the tendency of water to move. Water always moves from higher to lower Ψ. It has two parts: <b>solute potential</b> Ψs = −iCRT (always zero or negative; more solute means lower) and <b>pressure potential</b> Ψp (positive in a turgid plant cell, zero in an open beaker). Ψ = Ψp + Ψs. Pure water in an open container has Ψ = 0.',
  'Here <i>i</i> is the ionization constant (1 for sucrose, 2 for NaCl), C is molar concentration, R = 0.0831 L·bar/(mol·K) and T is in kelvin (°C + 273). The result is in bars. A plant cell in pure water takes in water until Ψp rises enough to make the cell Ψ equal to 0, so no net movement: turgor.'],
 ('we compared solute concentrations to guess where water goes','that pressure also changes water movement, which matters for plants','how to calculate the direction and size of the driving force','Ψ = Ψp + Ψs and Ψs = −iCRT'),
 [('Ψ = Ψp + Ψs','total water potential'),('Ψs = −iCRT','R = 0.0831 L·bar/mol·K'),('i: sucrose 1, NaCl 2','ionization constant'),('water moves high Ψ → low Ψ','always')],
 [lab('wp-lab','A cell in a beaker',canvas='wp-canvas',aria='A plant cell in a beaker. Bars show the water potential of the cell and of the solution and an arrow shows which way water moves.',
   seg=('wp-i','Solute in the beaker',[('1','sucrose (i = 1)'),('2','NaCl (i = 2)')]),
   ctl=[('wp-cc','Cell solute',0,1,.05,.4),('wp-p','Cell pressure Ψp (bar)',0,10,.5,0),('wp-cs','Beaker solute (M)',0,1,.05,.1)],
   pre='<p class="small" style="margin:0">Cell solute is sucrose (i = 1); beaker is open (Ψp = 0); 25 °C.</p>',
   out=[('wp-cpsi','cell Ψ','x'),('wp-spsi','beaker Ψ','a'),('wp-dir','net water movement','v')],own=True)],
 [('Cell solute 0.4 M, pressure 0, beaker 0.1 M sucrose. Where does water go? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#wp-cc",.4],["#wp-p",0],["#wp-cs",.1],["click","#wp-i [data-k=\"1\"]"]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','Cell Ψ = −9.9 bar; beaker Ψ = −2.5 bar. Water moves into the cell (toward lower Ψ).'),
  ('Raise the cell pressure until the cell Ψ equals the beaker Ψ. What is the pressure? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#wp-p",7.5]) + '\'>Try 7.5</button>','','About 7.4 bar (−9.9 + 7.4 = −2.5). Water stops entering: the cell is turgid.')],
 ('A plant cell has solute concentration 0.3 M sucrose and Ψp = 0 at 25 °C. It is placed in 0.1 M sucrose. Find each Ψ and the direction of water movement.',[
  '<b>Constants.</b> R = 0.0831, T = 298 K, RT ≈ 24.8 L·bar/mol; i = 1.',
  '<b>Cell.</b> Ψs = −(1)(0.3)(24.8) = −7.4 bar; Ψ = 0 + (−7.4) = −7.4 bar.',
  '<b>Beaker.</b> Ψs = −(1)(0.1)(24.8) = −2.5 bar; open so Ψp = 0 and Ψ = −2.5 bar.',
  '<b>Direction.</b> Water moves from −2.5 (higher) to −7.4 (lower): <b>into the cell</b>.']),
 ['Forgetting the negative sign on Ψs.','Using °C instead of kelvin.','Saying water moves toward higher Ψ.','Forgetting i for ionic solutes such as NaCl.','Thinking pressure potential is always zero. It is zero in an open container, positive in a turgid cell.'],
 ('What the FRQ asks',['Most questions give concentrations, ask for Ψ of each side, then ask which way water moves and what happens to the cell. Show the substitution with units, state the direction in words, and for plant cells mention the role of Ψp (turgor).']),
 [('A solution has a higher solute concentration. Compared with pure water, its solute potential is',['more negative','less negative','equal','positive'],0,'Ψs = −iCRT becomes more negative as C increases.')])

mod('activetr','Active transport & bulk transport',
 'Moving a substance down its gradient is free. Moving it up the gradient costs energy, and cells spend a lot of ATP doing it.',
 ['<b>Facilitated diffusion</b> uses channel or carrier proteins but still moves substances down their concentration gradient: no energy needed. <b>Active transport</b> uses energy (usually ATP) and a protein pump to move a substance against its gradient. The <b>sodium-potassium pump</b> moves 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in per ATP, keeping Na⁺ low and K⁺ high inside the cell. <b>Cotransport</b> uses the gradient built by a pump to move a second substance (for example glucose with Na⁺).',
  'Large molecules and particles move by vesicles: <b>endocytosis</b> brings material in (phagocytosis for particles, receptor-mediated for specific molecules) and <b>exocytosis</b> sends it out. These also use energy.'],
 ('all transport was either diffusion or osmosis','that gradients can be built and used, and that cells can move substances uphill','how nerve cells, kidney cells and plant roots accumulate substances','facilitated diffusion, active transport, cotransport and vesicles'),
 [('passive: down gradient, no ATP','simple and facilitated diffusion'),('active: against gradient, ATP','pumps'),('Na⁺/K⁺ pump: 3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in','per ATP'),('endocytosis / exocytosis','bulk transport')],
 [stagelab('at-lab','The sodium-potassium pump',canvas='at-canvas',aria='A pump protein in the membrane cycling through six steps, moving sodium out and potassium in.',p='np',n=6),
  lab('at-lab2','Passive or active?',canvas='at-canvas2',aria='Two compartments separated by a membrane with a channel; particles move depending on the mode and on ATP.',
   seg=('at-mode','Carrier',[('fd','facilitated diffusion (channel)'),('ap','active pump')]),
   pre='<div class="row"><button type="button" class="btn primary" id="at-atp">ATP: on</button><button type="button" class="btn" id="at-reset">Start over</button></div>',
   out=[('at-l','left side','x'),('at-r','right side','a'),('at-st','what is happening','')],own=True)],
 [('Run the pump lab to the stage where ATP is used. What does it do? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#np-st",1]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','ATP transfers a phosphate to the pump, changing its shape so that the sodium binding site faces outside.'),
  ('Choose facilitated diffusion and run it, then try the active pump with ATP off. What differs? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#at-mode [data-k=fd]"]) + '\'>Facilitated</button>','','Facilitated diffusion evens out the two sides without ATP. The pump moves particles to the side that already has more only when ATP is on; with ATP off it does nothing.')],
 ('A cell has 10 mM K⁺ outside and 140 mM inside. K⁺ moves in through the Na⁺/K⁺ pump. Is this passive or active? Explain.',[
  '<b>Gradient.</b> K⁺ is higher inside (140) than outside (10).',
  '<b>Direction.</b> It moves in, toward the higher concentration: against the gradient.',
  '<b>Conclusion.</b> Movement against a gradient requires energy: this is active transport using ATP.']),
 ['Thinking facilitated diffusion uses ATP.','Saying active transport always moves substances in. It can move them out (Na⁺).','Mixing up the 3 and 2 in the sodium-potassium pump.','Forgetting that vesicle transport also requires energy.','Saying channel proteins pump ions. They let ions through down a gradient.'],
 ('Spotting the type',['Ask: which way relative to the gradient, and does it need a protein or ATP? Down the gradient and no protein: simple diffusion. Down the gradient with a protein: facilitated diffusion. Up the gradient: active transport. Large particle in a vesicle: endo- or exocytosis.']),
 [('A substance moves from low to high concentration through a protein. This is',['active transport','facilitated diffusion','simple diffusion','osmosis'],0,'Against the gradient with a protein and energy: active transport.')])

mod('origin','Origins of cell compartments',
 'Mitochondria and chloroplasts look like small bacteria living inside our cells, and the evidence says that is close to what they are.',
 ['The <b>endosymbiotic theory</b> proposes that mitochondria arose when an ancestral cell engulfed an aerobic bacterium that survived and became a permanent partner, and that chloroplasts arose later when a cell engulfed a photosynthetic cyanobacterium. The nucleus and endomembrane system probably arose by infolding of the plasma membrane.',
  'Evidence: both organelles have a <b>double membrane</b>, their own <b>circular DNA</b>, their own ribosomes similar to bacterial ribosomes, and they reproduce by <b>binary fission</b> independently of the cell. Prokaryotes lack membrane-bound organelles and have a nucleoid instead of a nucleus; eukaryotes have both.'],
 ('organelles were just parts of the cell','that some organelles were once free-living cells','why mitochondria and chloroplasts have their own DNA and ribosomes','endosymbiosis and its evidence'),
 [('mitochondrion: from an aerobic bacterium','engulfed'),('chloroplast: from a cyanobacterium','engulfed later'),('evidence: own circular DNA, 70S-type ribosomes, double membrane, binary fission','organelle features'),('nucleus and ER: from infolded membrane','proposed')],
 [stagelab('or-lab','How a eukaryotic cell may have arisen',canvas='or-canvas',aria='Four stages: an ancestral cell, membrane infolding, engulfing an aerobic bacterium, and engulfing a cyanobacterium.',p='or',n=4)],
 [('Go to stage 3. What does the engulfed bacterium become? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#or-st",2]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','A mitochondrion: it provides ATP to the host and gets a protected, nutrient-rich home.'),
  ('Go to stage 4. How does this change the cell? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#or-st",3]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','The engulfed cyanobacterium becomes a chloroplast, so the cell can make its own sugars: the ancestor of algae and plants.')],
 ('A researcher finds that an organelle has circular DNA, ribosomes that are sensitive to an antibiotic that stops bacterial protein synthesis, and divides by binary fission. What can be inferred?',[
  '<b>Compare with bacteria.</b> Those are all bacterial features.',
  '<b>Inference.</b> The organelle likely descended from a free-living bacterium that was engulfed: the endosymbiotic theory.',
  '<b>Strength.</b> This is evidence consistent with the theory, not proof; combine it with DNA sequence similarity for stronger support.']),
 ['Saying mitochondria and chloroplasts were made by the nucleus.','Thinking every organelle came from endosymbiosis. The nucleus and ER are thought to come from infolding.','Saying prokaryotes have no DNA.','Writing that the theory has been proven rather than strongly supported.','Mixing up which organelle came first (mitochondria first in the usual account).'],
 ('Evidence language',['Use "consistent with" for evidence. Name at least two features and link each to bacteria: circular DNA, bacterial-type ribosomes, binary fission, double membrane (from engulfing).']),
 [('Which feature of mitochondria best supports the endosymbiotic theory?',['they have their own circular DNA and ribosomes','they are in animal cells','they make ATP','they have folded membranes'],0,'Own circular DNA and bacterial-type ribosomes resemble free-living bacteria.')])
